WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.029
Mel Gibson hasn't exactly had the smoothest
couple of decades.

00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:08.700
He's got something of a controversial past
to say the least, including numerous allegations

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.200
of racism, bigotry, and domestic abuse.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:15.990
Although there was once a time when any Mel
Gibson film was pretty much guaranteed to

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:20.660
draw a crowd, that's not really the case nowadays,
and it's clear that the acclaimed actor and

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.400
Oscar-winning director's star has fallen very
far indeed.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:25.690
"Oh, you wanna see crazy?

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:26.840
I'll show you-"

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:33.610
Nonetheless, Gibson now looks to be making
strides toward a comeback.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:38.879
The internet is awash with praise for Gibson's
latest movie appearance in Boss Level, a sci-fi

00:00:38.879 --> 00:00:40.920
action Hulu original.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.730
Gibson isn't the film's lead, and he wasn't
behind the helm as its director either, but

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:49.609
fans and critics alike are absolutely loving
the flick, with some taking special note of

00:00:49.609 --> 00:00:50.609
his performance.

00:00:50.609 --> 00:00:55.589
By all accounts, Boss Level is a fun and gleefully
violent action film that joins the ever-growing

00:00:55.589 --> 00:00:59.050
list of movies to have utilized the time-loop
story trope.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:04.589
Frank Grillo, probably most familiar for his
role as Crossbones in the MCU, stars in Boss

00:01:04.589 --> 00:01:09.550
Level as Roy Pulver, a Special Forces soldier
stuck in an endless time loop, forced to relive

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:12.010
the day of his death over and over again.

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:16.860
Meanwhile, Naomi Watts plays Roy's estranged
wife Jemma, and her boss is the defense contractor

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.750
Colonel Clive Ventor, played by Gibson himself.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:25.020
In the midst of Roy's many time loops, a suspicious
death puts Ventor on his radar — and from

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.340
there, the two are set on a collision course
filled with all the explosions, car chases,

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.700
and gunfights that you could possibly hope
for.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.210
Now, it's true that both casual movie-lovers
and professional critics seem to agree that

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:40.750
Gibson's new movie isn't exactly breaking
new ground when it comes to the time-loop

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:41.830
sub-genre.

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:46.600
That said, Boss Level is still unique among
such films for just how fun and unapologetically

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:47.900
bloody it can get.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:52.330
As a result, plenty of viewers have taken
to Twitter to praise the new sci-fi action

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:53.330
flick.

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:54.330
One user told their followers:

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:59.450
"If you like stuff like Groundhog Day, Russian
Doll, Happy Death Day(2 u), then check out

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:00.450
Boss Level on Hulu."

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.090
Meanwhile, another Twitter user called the
movie:

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:07.420
"A hugely entertaining time loop action film
[...[ bloody and inventive with its tongue

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.159
placed firmly in cheek."

00:02:09.159 --> 00:02:13.340
That same user also pointed out that Mel Gibson
ought to have been given a little more to

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:17.340
do in the movie — which is pretty telling
praise in a world where many would agree that

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.160
less Mel Gibson is definitely better than
more.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.120
Putting a little smack talk twist on their
praise for the movie, another Twitterer said:

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.709
"BOSS LEVEL is like if GUNS AKIMBO was Good."

00:02:26.709 --> 00:02:31.019
And, although they didn't give a full verdict
on the movie itself, one user highlighted

00:02:31.019 --> 00:02:35.879
the sheer bloodiness of Boss Level — and
especially Frank Grillo's endless, gory deaths

00:02:35.879 --> 00:02:36.879
— writing:

00:02:36.879 --> 00:02:39.249
"My theory as to why Frank Grillo made Boss
Level...

00:02:39.249 --> 00:02:43.349
To dethrone Sean Bean with the most on screen
deaths with just 1 movie."

00:02:43.349 --> 00:02:46.760
Surprisingly enough, it isn't just the fans
who are loving Boss Level, either.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:51.480
While the film's current Rotten Tomatoes score
of 72 percent indicates that not all the critics

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:56.230
loved the movie, it's still managed to garner
some pretty noteworthy critical praise.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:59.150
At IndieWire, critic Christian Blauvelt wrote
that:

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:03.359
"Grillo gives Boss Level the thing most video
game movie riffs lack: a pulse."

00:03:03.359 --> 00:03:06.029
While Gary Goldstein of the Los Angeles Times
said:

00:03:06.029 --> 00:03:10.750
"[Boss Level] takes a well-worn gag and injects
energy, showing the genre is still a game

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:11.750
worth playing."

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:14.370
Writing for Den of Geek, Bernard Boo said
of the movie:

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:18.700
"It's a lot of fun to watch and one of the
best showcases of Grillo's 'very particular

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:20.120
set of skills' yet."

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:24.469
While Decider's Johnny Loftus was more economical
with his words, writing simply:

00:03:24.469 --> 00:03:25.660
"It's outrageous.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:26.660
Just go with it."

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:30.349
So it really does seem like Boss Level might
be something of a surprise hit — and at

00:03:30.349 --> 00:03:33.159
least some of that is down to Mel Gibson himself.

00:03:33.159 --> 00:03:37.430
Just what it means for his troublesome career,
however, remains to be seen.

